\begin{abstract}
% A space is needed before the text starts so that the first paragraph
% is indented properly.

Snoring is a common source of sleep disturbance that negatively impacts partner sleep quality and relationship well-being. Existing mitigation approaches, including continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) devices, nasal strips, and earplugs, either require wearable hardware or provide limited attenuation of the low-frequency acoustic energy characteristic of snoring.

This thesis proposes a non-wearable acoustic noise mitigation system using near-field microphone and loudspeaker array processing to actively reduce perceived snoring at a listener’s ear. The system employs adaptive beamforming and active noise control techniques to generate a spatially localized anti-noise field directed toward the sleeping partner while minimizing disturbance to the surrounding environment. Near-field array modeling enables improved low-frequency cancellation performance compared to conventional passive methods.

Experimental results demonstrate localized snore attenuation and improved low-frequency suppression, indicating the feasibility of targeted acoustic cancellation for sleep environments without requiring wearable devices.

\end{abstract}


